{"id": "81509cf3-35b0-432b-a2d1-e62595253a22", "timestamp": "2026-10-25T00:02:00.749871", "source": "user", "parent_id": "a44b0df4-6c46-4d60-bc3d-a6567f24f9c2", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Thanks. Before we design anything, please do a read-only inspection pass and report back — no experiment yet.\\n\\n1) Inspect /workspace and identify the training codebase. Specifically locate the zero-bubble / pipeline-parallel scheduling code: flags like --zero-bubble-max-pending-backward (ZB-2p), --zero-bubble-pipeline-schedule or similar (ZB-V, ZB-1p, ZB-2p variants), plus the scheduler implementation that emits the microbatch/segment order.\\n2) Report what observability exists: does the scheduler or the codebase expose a way to measure \\\"bubble rate\\\" (e.g., rank idle time, profiler traces, timeline/tensorboard hooks, virtual stage or micro-scheduler logs)? Note whether any GPU profiling tooling (nsys/torch.profiler) appears to be wired in.\\n3) Report which model/pipeline-parallel configurations the repo plausibly supports for a multi-GPU pipeline run (stage count, microbatch count, model size knobs), and whether the repo appears runnable at all in this environment (deps, GPU availability — note our runtime has no GPU and no network, so anything we design is design-only).\\n4) Return your proposed experimental approach: how we would activate ZB-2p, what we would profile, what we would define as \\\"bubble rate\\\" operationally, and what the comparison baseline would be.\\n\\nThen state your consequential assumptions and uncertainties explicitly. Please distinguish clearly between what you actually observed in the repository and what you are assuming.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"Does the automatic scheduling algorithm’s output (ZB-2p) truly achieve a near-zero bubble rate in practice when profiled on mul