shot-allreduce, --disable-bucketing-for-gradient-scattering / --disable-gradient-bucketizer, --pp-bubbles-left, --attention-dropout, and ZB-specific: --zero-bubble-pipeline-schedule {ZB-H, ZB-V, ZB-1p, ZB-2p} or --zero-bubble-max-pending-backward N.\n- Observability: --use-pytorch-profiler, --profile, --profile-step-start/-end, --tensorboard-dir, --timing-log-level, timers('forward-backward'), --log-throughput, Megatron's `--log-progress`, plus potential schedule-dump logging in ZB code (e.g., virtual-stages logs / `get_schedule` prints).\n\nPlan proposal — key design idea: two-tier measurement.\nTier A (CPU-only, runnable-if-repo-present): schedule extraction & analytical bubble computation. Monkeypatch/hook the scheduler to dump the event list (rank, time-slot, microbatch, fwd/bwd/wgrad), then compute bubble rate = idle slots/(total slots) on the schedule graph. This is deterministic, cheap, reproducible, and directly tests \"does the algorithm's output have zero bubble.\"\nTier B (multi-GPU, requires hardware unavailable here): real profile with torch.profiler/NSYS on 8×GPU; measure per-rank busy/idle from CUDA activity traces aligned to schedule boundaries.\n\nOperational definition of bubble rate:\n- Schedule-level (analytical): B_sched = 1 - (Σ mandatory compute work)/(P × T_makespan) using slots; also per-rank idle fraction.\n- System-level: B_wall = (1/P) Σ_r (T_step - busy_time_r)/T_step where busy_time = union of fwd/bwd/wgrad kernel intervals; plus throughput proxy: TFLOP-per-GPU achieved / per-GPU peak-without-PP.\nKey: distinguish \"structural bubble\" (0 for ZB-2p under uniform-compute assumption) from \"effective bubble\" (>0 due to comm, memory-driven recompute, stragglers, uneven layers, warmup flush overlap).\n\nHypotheses/predictions: schedule-level bubble ≈ 0; system-level bubble materially > 0 (paper itself reports ~20-34% throughput gains, not P× speedup), so \"near-zero in practice\" is expected false/only-true-in-the-schedule-graph. Also ZB-2p has memory overhead (~1.19×1F1B?) and extra weight-gradient communication; the paper reports ZB-H needs 1.19× memory of 1F1B.\n\nMemory: ZB-2p: memory = (1 + something). Paper: ZB-H(2P) reduces bubble to zero with 1.19× memory overhead vs 1F1B's baseline? Actually paper says ZB-H has 1.19× memory overhead of 1F1B and ZB-1P has 1P. I'll mention approximate figures as \"to verify from the paper/repo docs\" rather than asserting.\n\nBaselines: 1F1B, interleaved 1F1B (VPP), ZB-1p, ZB-V; and a theoretical lower bound.\n\nConcurrency/confound controls: same model config, same seeds, fixed num layers divisible by stages, mock data, NCCL_ALGO fixed, disable other overlaps or hold them constant, run ≥ N steps, discard warmup, randomize order, isolate clocks (persistence mode, locked clocks), no other tenants, use --num-layers-per-virtual-pipeline-stage identical across arms where applicable, ensure microbatch count ≥ ... For ZB-2p, num_microbatches must be ≥ something; also 2P pending backward requires global batch size ≥ mbs*dp*(2P)... need to note constraint: max_pending_backward=2*stages per the task note.\n\nFeasible checks here: none beyond static inspection; we cannot even do static inspection because repo absent. So the plan includes a \"gating\" step: obtain the repo (blocked: no network) → the experiment must remain design-only, or researcher must provide repo path.\n\nUncertainties/consequential choices to raise with researcher:\n1. Flag semantics: --zero-bubble-max-pending-backward=2P vs a named schedule flag --zero-bubble-pipeline-schedule=ZB-2p. Which repo version (NVIDIA Megatron-LM ZB branch vs NeMo vs Megatron-Core main)? Task note says \"for example\", so we should verify by --help.\n2. Definition of bubble rate: schedule-graph idle slots vs wall-clock rank idle. This is the crux of answering the question.\n3. Profiling overhead perturbation: CUPTI/nsys overhead inflates idle. Mitigate with event-based rank timestamps and a no-profiler control run (NCCL-based heartbeat/timing logs).\n4. Whether \"in practice\" includes communication and non-uniform layers.\n5. Hardware unavailable → decision: design-only deliverable vs. wait for GPU access.\n\nBudget: 6 questions available, 60 steps.\n\nLet me write the handoff now. It should be well-organized, with observation vs assumption clearly separated.\n\nAlso note: /usr/local/cuda exists as dir but bin listing gave nothing — probably broken symlink. Let me quickly verify to be accurate. Also check for a Megatron wheel in pip cache. Let's do a couple more quick checks to be precise.\n", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "ls -la /usr/local/ | head; echo \"--- cuda dir ---\"; ls -la /usr/local/cuda 2>&1 | head -5; echo \"--- pip cache/site-packages ---\"; python3 -c \"import site,sys;print(site.getsitepackages())\"; ls /usr/lib/python3/dist-packages 2>/dev/null | grep -iE \"torch|megatron|numpy|cupy\" || echo \"no ML pkgs in dist-packages\"", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-aed2e19ba696f098", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-aed2e19ba696f098", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"command\": \"ls -la /usr/local/ | head; echo \\\"--- cuda dir ---\\\"; ls -la /usr/local/cuda 2>&1 | head -5; echo \\\"--- pip cache/site-packages ---\\\"; python3 -c \\\"import site,sys;print(site.getsitepackages())\\\"; ls /usr/lib/python3/dist-packages 2>/dev/null | grep -iE \\\"torch|megatron|numpy|cupy\\\" || echo \\\"no ML pkgs in dist-packages\\\"\"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-a4810310-1686-4e8e-a327-a49b7ad70bd5", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "execute_check: {\"command\": \"ls -la /usr/local/ | head; echo \\\"--- cuda dir ---\\\"; ls -la /usr/local/cuda 2>&1 | head -5; echo \\\"--- pip cache/site-packages ---\\\"; python3 -c \\\"import site,sys;print(site.getsitepackages())\