1F1B baseline), `forward_backward_pipelining_with_interleaving` (VPP), plus any ZB entry point (`forward_backward_pipelining_hybrid`, `_ZB*`, `schedule_table`, `virtual_microbatches`, `get_num_microbatches`).\n- The ZB generator itself is usually a **pure-CPU table builder**: it emits a per-stage list of `(microbatch, FWD|BWD|WGRAD|IDLE)` slots. This is the single most important fact for a CPU-only environment: **the schedule order is computable without GPUs.**\n\n## 2) Observability (observed: none present here; assumed available in upstream Megatron)\n- `--profile` + `--profile-step-start/-end` → nsys/NVTX ranges (`forward-backward`, `forward-compute`, `backward-compute`, `loss-scale`, `optimizer`); `--use-pytorch-profiler` → `torch.profiler` chrome/TB trace; `--tensorboard-dir`, `--timing-log-level`, `--log-throughput`, and per-stage `timers(...)`.\n- Not observed anywhere: a first-class \"bubble rate\" metric. **Bubble rate must be derived**, either from the schedule table (analytic) or from rank timelines (profiled).\n- **No profiling tool is wired in here** (no nsys, no torch, no GPU) → GPU profiling is design-only in this environment.\n\n## 3) Plausible configs & runnability\n- Upstream-consistent knobs: `--pipeline-model-parallel-size` (2/4/8/16), `--num-layers-per-virtual-pipeline-stage` (VPP factor), `--micro-batch-size`/`--global-batch-size` (⇒ `num_microbatches M`), `--num-layers/--hidden-size/--num-attention-heads/--seq-length`, `--mock-data` for hardware-free dry runs, `--transformer-impl transformer_engine` for ZB's one-shot-allreduce path.\n- **Runnability here: none for real runs.** Design-only, as the environment note says. What *is* runnable in principle (if the repo were mounted) is the schedule-table generator alone, on CPU, since it is combinatorial — I'd gate the design on verifying that.\n\n## 4) Proposed experimental approach\n\n**Core idea: measure \"bubble rate\" at two distinct levels and require the claim to pass both.** The task's claim